00:13:33.483 --> 00:13:35.097
<v Dan North>And we all have a lot of pie.

00:13:35.097 --> 00:13:36.378
<v Mike Hall>Yeah. Which is awesome.

00:13:36.378 --> 00:13:37.436
<v Dan North>Because pie's good.

00:13:37.436 --> 00:14:12.236
<v Mike Hall>Yeah. Okay. Well, going to so many different conferences and speaking to different types of audiences, one of the things I've talked to some speakers about is how do they prepare and how do they maybe change the messages? Or how do you or do you change your message when you're talking to a predominantly job audience or predominantly agile practitioners, people who identify themselves as agile people and they come. Do you, when you are going to be talking about your topics, do you adjust what you're going to say for the audience or do you say, I have a message and I'm going to just keep repeating that specific message?

00:14:12.236 --> 00:16:49.476
<v Dan North>So, now who was it? I think it was someone like Rebecca Parsons is the CTO at ThoughtWorks. She said to me, when I was talking about BDD and I said to her, I feel like I've been talking about BDD forever. And she said, if you've got an idea and it's a good idea, you'll just find that you keep saying it again and again and again and again because people need to hear it again and again and again and you're going to get lots of different audiences and you're going to keep saying that message. Now, that's if you're trying to get a particular idea across. What I find is, and I was looking back, I've got like, I don't know, 40 or 50 different talks I've given over about, you know, eight years or something across all kinds of different topics and what I'll try and do, even if I'm giving the same talk again or the same, you know, title and abstract kind of thing, so it's the same, it's the rough, it's largely the same message, I'll always try and refresh it. And because it may be that, you know, if it's a talk I gave six months ago, my thinking's moved on. And I look at it and I go, do I still think those things? Or have I tuned that message? Or actually this thing came out a bit ambiguous and so I want to kind of tweak it. So I'm constantly tweaking my talks, constantly tweaking, well, yeah, so trying to move the ideas forward. I mean, I had a massive, I think epiphany is probably the right word, in the last couple of years with the Accelerated Agile stuff. So I went from being a big A Agile consultant in a big A Agile consulting firm. I mean, ThoughtWorks doesn't have a named methodology, if you like. They'll use whatever works in a situation, they're very pragmatic in that way. But there was very much a ThoughtWorks feel of doing Agile. And the kind of clients I'll be working with, these big, heavy, enterprising organizations where you needed to move, you know, help them move at the speed they could move at. And then I ended up in this tiny little team doing crazy stuff. And had to rethink a whole bunch of stuff. So I was, you know, you say the iconoclast thing. There were a whole bunch of, like, for me, real core truths that I sincerely believed about how software gets written that I've found myself challenging. And what I try and do in those situations. So you can either just go, la, la, la, I'm going to go back in my, you know, in my happy place. Or try and assimilate that. And so that's where I've ended up now. And someone was asking me about, like, you know, why have I called it Accelerated Agile and not be past Agile? And isn't there time for a new thing? And I think the main discovery I've made, or the main realization I've come to over the last couple of years is the thing I've been working with and the thing I've been, you know, that I'm now trying to document is Agile.

00:16:49.476 --> 00:16:49.810
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:53.151
<v Dan North>As in the manifesto rather than Agile as in the methodology.

00:16:53.151 --> 00:16:53.485
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:16:53.485 --> 00:16:57.048
<v Dan North>You know, the methodologies. So get back to the core principles.

00:16:57.048 --> 00:16:57.661
<v Mike Hall>Yeah, yeah.

00:16:57.661 --> 00:17:19.766
<v Dan North>And, you know, and people over process suggests that people and interactions are more important than any process. Ergo, as soon as you start defining that as a process, you've systemically set up to fail. Right. You know, there is a dogma around Scrum. There is a dogma around Kanban. There is a dogma around BDD. There is a dogma around all these different things. XP. XP had the 12 commandments.

00:17:19.766 --> 00:17:20.100
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:25.890
<v Dan North>Now, you talk to Ken Beck about XP, and that's the opposite of anything he wanted people to be thinking.

