00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:42.093
<v Dan North>Right. He's very much, he's entirely values driven. You know, he's very much, like the whole point for him, the whole point of the first book was, you know, communication, simplicity, feedback, or the core values. Oh, by the way, here's some things he did on this really cool project.

00:17:42.093 --> 00:17:44.710
<v Mike Hall>Yeah, here's some nice tools that support that.

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:58.853
<v Dan North>And it was experiential. It was like, we've done this, and these are the things that we found that worked. Now, you have to do this. Right. And then that became, you know, and he also had this lovely picture of how they support each other and which ones.

00:17:58.853 --> 00:17:59.131
<v Mike Hall>Yeah.

00:17:59.131 --> 00:18:35.100
<v Dan North>It was a pattern diagram. It was a pattern, a diagram of resolving forces and patterns. Like, he thinks in patterns. He's one of those guys. And, unfortunately, people wanted rules. And the point about a pattern is it's contextual. And the point about rules is they're not. And so people took all these patterns, all these really good ideas, like TDD, like pair programming. It's brilliant in many situations. It's not a thing you should, you know, there are no silver bullets, right, including that. Automation. Automation has an evil dark side. As soon as you automate something, you're locking that thing. You're now making that thing brittle.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:35.435
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:18:35.435 --> 00:18:41.782
<v Dan North>Yeah. Automation has a problem, which may be okay. Maybe you want fast and repeatable at the expense of malleable.

00:18:41.782 --> 00:18:42.172
<v Mike Hall>Mm-hmm.

00:18:42.172 --> 00:18:43.731
<v Dan North>But it is at the expense of.

00:18:43.731 --> 00:18:45.958
<v Mike Hall>Right, right. There's always trade-offs.

00:18:45.958 --> 00:18:55.702
<v Dan North>Yeah. And all of these things. And so, dogma, I mean, bringing right back to your opening question, I think dogma comes from where we don't have context or where we have fear.

00:18:55.702 --> 00:18:56.092
<v Mike Hall>Mm-hmm.

00:18:56.092 --> 00:19:08.397
<v Dan North>And I think those two things tend to be very closely related. So the more context we have, the more experience we have, the more we understand a situation, the less fearful we are. Or the less irrationally fearful we are.

